  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Michel Della Negra",
          "Peter Jenni",
          "Tejinder S. Virdee"
        ],
        "citation": "For the discovery of the Higgs boson by the ATLAS and CMS experiments."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B019"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B003 as my preferred configuration. Independent ATLAS and CMS observations, followed by measurements across production and decay channels, make the discovery both sharply identifiable and mature. That strength is scientific; the proposed credit still needs to be justified by the historical record of the two collaborations.",
    "case_against": "This was a collective achievement by thousands. Naming Della Negra, Jenni, and Virdee should recognize their leadership in building and guiding the experiments without suggesting that they alone made the discovery. The committee should verify how their distinct contributions support this attribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors circuit QED, arguing that its theoretical framework and experimental realization form a specific, repeatedly used platform; he sees its foundational pairing as more tightly joined than quantum error correction.",
        "response": "The close link between theory and realization is a strength of B035, and the repeated use of the platform supports its maturity. I still prefer B003 because ATLAS and CMS independently observed the same predicted particle, with later measurements testing it across multiple channels. B035’s attribution across theory and experimental development also requires historical review."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors optical lattice clocks, citing independently demonstrated precision and tests of fundamental physics; he argues that continuing improvements need not delay recognition of the established method.",
        "response": "I agree that the clocks’ established precision and demonstrated scientific use make them a serious candidate; future applications need not be settled first. The remaining question is how narrowly to define the foundational contribution amid continued work across many groups. For B003, the discovery itself has a particularly clear boundary, though its individual credit remains difficult."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that the demonstrated control of ultracold atoms in optical lattices is awardable on its own, even while their reach as predictive simulators for real materials remains open.",
        "response": "That distinction is persuasive: a controlled many-body platform can be scientifically valuable without already predicting the behavior of real materials. Its broad history across theory and experimental milestones still makes the prize scope and attribution harder to settle. I retain B003 because the discovery is more specifically bounded, while recognizing that the collaboration-wide credit must be handled carefully."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The relative leadership contributions of Della Negra, Jenni, and Virdee across ATLAS and CMS, and how a three-person award can represent the wider collaborations accurately, need careful historical assessment."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "The supplied profile notes my close collaboration with Anne L'Huillier, a 2023 laureate; she is not among the credited people in my preferred configuration or alternatives. My atomic-physics background is adjacent to B006 and B019, but the supplied profile identifies no direct collaboration or personal connection to their credited people."
  }
}
